/*!
@defgroup dsc_tro TRO: Tracking Ring Oscillator
@details The MCUXpresso SDK provides a driver for the Tracking Ring Oscillator (TRO).

The TRO's frequency tracks process, voltage, and temperature, so reading it works as an on-die PVT
sensor. Four delay lines (standard, long, complex gate, RC) can each be lengthened or shortened to
match the loop to a real critical path rather than to a fixed delay.

# Function groups

## Initialization
TRO_Init() enables the TRO bus clock (unless driver clock control is disabled), sets CSR[TROEN],
waits for CSR[TRO_CONFIG_VLD], then writes the delay line and divider registers - writes issued
before TRO_CONFIG_VLD asserts are discarded by hardware. It fails with kStatus_Fail if CSR[LK] is
already set. The caller must first select a running reference clock
(SIM_PERIPH_CLKSEL0[TRO_CLK_SEL]) and, on dual-core devices, set bus ownership
(kSIM_AIPS_SEL_TRO); this driver never touches those shared SIM registers.

TRO_Deinit() clears CSR[TROEN] and gates the bus clock back off. Hardware resets DLY_CFG and
DIV_CFG when TROEN clears, so a later TRO_Init() must supply the configuration again, and no TRO
register - not even the read-only counters - is accessible until then.

## Oscillator configuration
TRO_SetDelayLineConfig() and TRO_SetClockDivider() each poll a DONE bit before writing, because the
configuration is shifted into hardware as a transfer and writes issued mid-transfer are discarded.

There is no set-frequency API: the delay-line-to-frequency mapping is unmeasured per part (reset
state pairs maximum code with a mid-range frequency), so sweep TRO_SetDelayLineConfig() and
measure with window mode to build one.

TRO_LockRegisters() sets or clears CSR[LK]. While locked, every other write API in this driver is
silently ineffective and still reports success; check TRO_IsLocked() if that matters.

## Window mode
Window mode counts tro_clk cycles over a window of reference clock cycles and captures the count
into CNT_SNAPSHOT, comparing it against WIN_MAX/WIN_MIN. TRO_SetWindowConfig() disables window
mode before writing the size and thresholds, since hardware only accepts those while disabled;
arming is always an explicit TRO_EnableWindowMode() call. TRO_SetWindowSize() does the same for
just the size, without touching CSR[TROEN] or the delay/divider configuration.

Resolution in Hz is 1/window-duration and does not depend on the reference frequency. Maximum
useful window is bounded by the 22-bit snapshot, roughly 29 ms at 144 MHz. The driver does no
snapshot-to-Hz conversion; that is snapshot * refClkFreq / windowSize, left to the caller because
it needs a 64-bit intermediate and this driver cannot validate the reference frequency.

## Interrupts and status
Window expired, above WIN_MAX, and below WIN_MIN share one interrupt vector and are
write-1-to-clear, so an ISR must check TRO_GetInterruptStatusFlags() rather than assume a single
cause. TRO_EnableInterrupts()/TRO_DisableInterrupts() read-modify-write WIN_CFG and are not
ISR-safe; TRO_GetInterruptStatusFlags()/TRO_ClearInterruptStatusFlags() only touch WIN_ST and are
ISR-safe. This driver supplies no interrupt handler.

## Phase comparator
The phase counter accumulates, once per reference clock period, the difference between the
observed tro_clk count and CSR[REF_CLK_MULT], so a small persistent drift integrates instead of
averaging out even though every individual window reports it as in tolerance.
TRO_SetReferenceClockMultiplier() sets the expected ratio; TRO_ClearPhaseCounter() writes a
self-clearing pulse. TRO_GetPhaseCounter() returns a sign-extended int32_t - do not mask it, that
would strip the sign.

CSR[REF_CLK_MULT] is 6 bits, so the phase comparator only works when tro_clk/refClk is an integer
ratio from 0 to 63.
*/
